{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And these are the kings who reigned in the land of Edom.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): these kings descended from Esav, and they were eight, and these are they: Bela son of Be'or, Yovav, Chusham, Hadad son of Bedad, Samlah, Shaul, Ba'al Chanand son of Akhbor, Hadar. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted them as corresponding to the eight times that Ya'akov said to Esav 'my lord' (אדני) at the beginning of the parashah: כה תאמרון לאדני לעשו, ואשלחה להגיד לאדני, מנחה היא שלוחה לאדני, למצא חן בעיני אדני, אדני יודע, יעבור נא אדני, עד אשר אבא אל אדני, למה זה אמצא חן בעיני אדני. And corresponding to them, a name of eight words was given to us in the verse ותמנע (Genesis 36:12) to prosecute him through them, and I have already mentioned this above. And these places that the parashah mentions for Edom are Botzrah and Teiman — Botzrah, as it is written (Isaiah 34): ״כי זבח לה׳ בבצרה וטבח גדול בארץ אדום״; the land of the Teimani, as it is written (Obadiah 9): ״וחתו גבוריך תימן למען יכרת איש מהר עשו מקטל״. And Scripture states that they did not have a king who was son of a king, as in Israel. And the parashah came to mention these kings to explain that the blessing of Esav was fulfilled, as it is written (Genesis 27): ״ועל חרבך תחיה״. And therefore Scripture lengthens the account of the kings and the chiefs who descended from him."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:18:05.131884087Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}